## A workspace held in memory

The cases of this chapter and the next run the compiler over looms held in memory, as the chapter "The compile layer" tests it, so no case touches a disk. The function `layerOf` gives the compiler such a workspace. Its `DocumentSource` reads each file from a record of paths and texts, and fails to read a path the record lacks, as storage fails for a loom that is not there. It lists the paths that end in `.loom` as the corpus, so a record may hold the workspace's configuration file beside its looms. Its `WorkspaceSettings` settles every loom on Loom's own anchor delimiters and TypeScript, with the variable `version` the workspace's configuration declares, and gives the workspace's `manifest`, or none when a case asks for a workspace without one. The function `withCompiler` runs one use of the compiler over such a workspace.

A loom written into this loom's code would be read as part of it, so each fixture is a list of its lines, and `lines` joins them into the text a loom holds. The function `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const manifest: WorkspaceManifest = {
  languages: { typescript: {} },
  corpus: 'corpus',
  variables: { version: '0.1.19' },
}

const layerOf = (
  files: Record<string, string>,
  configured: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest> = Option.some(manifest),
) =>
  Layer.provide(
    LoomCompiler.layer,
    Layer.mergeAll(
      FileSystemSource,
      LoomDiskCache.none,
      Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
        read: (path: string) =>
          path in files
            ? Effect.succeed(files[path] ?? '')
            : Effect.fail(new ReadError({ path, cause: 'no such loom' })),
        list: Option.some(() =>
          Effect.succeed(Object.keys(files).filter((path) => path.endsWith('.loom'))),
        ),
        hold: () => Effect.void,
        forget: () => Effect.void,
      }),
      Layer.succeed(WorkspaceSettings, {
        manifest: () => Effect.succeed(configured),
        resolve: () =>
          Effect.succeed({
            delims: defaultAnchorDelims,
            primaryLanguage: 'typescript',
            variables: { version: '0.1.19' },
            width: 100,
          }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const withCompiler = <A, E>(
  files: Record<string, string>,
  use: (compiler: LoomCompiler['Service']) => Effect.Effect<A, E>,
  configured: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest> = Option.some(manifest),
) => LoomCompiler.pipe(Effect.flatMap(use), Effect.provide(layerOf(files, configured)))

## Testing the outline

The cases run on Bun's test runner, with the `effectify` helper that lets a case be written as an Effect. They outline looms through the compiler's `outline` verb, which builds the corpus as the language server does, and list concepts through its `concepts` verb.

=>

import { describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { defaultAnchorDelims } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import type { WorkspaceManifest } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { DocumentSource, LoomCompiler } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { ReadError } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { WorkspaceSettings } from '../src/WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomDiskCache } from '../src/LoomDiskCache'
import { FileSystemSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'

const it = effectify(it_)

## The game the cases outline

The book of these cases is a game. Its book loom opens it and defines the concept `Room`, whose other form is `rooms`. Its chapter `Words` stands in Part I, The room, as its second chapter, and announces the part. Its opening prose links the concept by its other form, and a note a reviewer left follows it. Its section `Saying hello` writes the file `hello.ts`, declares a value warp in its prose, and holds code between two runs of prose. Its section `Installing it` switches to Bash and holds fenced code, whose first line would read as a heading. A discussion of Part I, `Waking`, holds one topic, with two messages and a conclusion. A loom with no frontmatter stands loose beside them.

=>

const files: Record<string, string> = {
  '/corpus/game.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Book: The game',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The game',
    '',
    'A game of rooms that wake one at a time.',
    '',
    '## Room {Concept}',
    '',
    'A room is a place the player stands in.',
    '',
    'Lexical forms:',
    '- rooms',
  ),
  '/corpus/words.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Part I: The room',
    'Chapter 2: Words',
    'Book: The game',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    '---',
    '',
    '# Words',
    '',
    `What the program says in its ${anchor('rooms')}.`,
    '',
    ':::',
    'Check the greeting with a reader.',
    ':::',
    '',
    '## Saying hello [hello.ts]',
    '',
    'The greeting {{ word = "hi" }} is short.',
    '',
    `=> export const hi = '${anchor('word')}'`,
    'export const twice = hi + hi',
    '',
    '~ After the code comes more prose.',
    '',
    '## Installing it {Bash}',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    '```',
    '# fetch the game',
    'curl -fsSL example.com',
    '```',
  ),
  '/corpus/waking.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Part I, Discussion 1: Waking',
    'Book: The game',
    '---',
    '',
    '# Waking',
    '',
    'What should wake a monster.',
    '',
    'What wakes a monster',
    '---',
    '',
    '~ Yegor',
    'What should wake a monster?',
    '',
    '~ Claude',
    "A trap's radius.",
    '',
    '::: {Conclusion}',
    'A trap going off wakes it.',
    ':::',
  ),
  '/corpus/notes.loom': lines('# Notes', '', 'Loose words.'),
}

const outlined = (path: string) => withCompiler(files, (compiler) => compiler.outline(path))

## A chapter in outline

The chapter `Words` is a chapter of the game, second in Part I, The room, and names TypeScript. Its opening section is its title's, at line seven counted from zero, with one hash. Its body holds its opening prose alone, since the note stands apart. The section `Saying hello` writes `hello.ts`, and its body runs prose, code, prose, with every mark taken out: the arrow before the first line of code and the tilde before the prose after it. The section `Installing it` switches to Bash, and its code arrives without its fence. The loom declares the variable `word` in `Saying hello`, and the workspace declares `version`.

=>

describe('the outline of a loom', () => {
  it.effect('outlines a chapter, its sections, and its variables', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const outline = yield* outlined('/corpus/words.loom')
      expect([outline.kind, outline.title, outline.book, outline.number, outline.language]).toEqual(
        ['Chapter', 'Words', 'The game', '2', 'TypeScript'],
      )
      expect(outline.part).toEqual({ number: 'I', name: 'The room' })
      expect(
        outline.sections.map((section) => [section.name, section.line, section.level]),
      ).toEqual([
        ['Words', 7, 1],
        ['Saying hello', 15, 2],
        ['Installing it', 24, 2],
      ])
      expect(outline.sections[0]?.body).toEqual([
        { _tag: 'Prose', text: `What the program says in its ${anchor('rooms')}.` },
      ])
      expect(outline.sections[0]?.notes).toEqual([{ text: 'Check the greeting with a reader.' }])
      expect(outline.sections[1]?.file).toBe('hello.ts')
      expect(outline.sections[1]?.body).toEqual([
        { _tag: 'Prose', text: 'The greeting {{ word = "hi" }} is short.' },
        {
          _tag: 'Code',
          text: `export const hi = '${anchor('word')}'\nexport const twice = hi + hi`,
        },
        { _tag: 'Prose', text: 'After the code comes more prose.' },
      ])
      expect([outline.sections[2]?.language, outline.sections[2]?.body]).toEqual([
        'Bash',
        [{ _tag: 'Code', text: '# fetch the game\ncurl -fsSL example.com' }],
      ])
      expect(outline.variables).toEqual([{ name: 'word', value: '"hi"', section: 'Saying hello' }])
      expect(outline.configuration).toEqual({ version: '0.1.19' })
    }),
  )

~

## A discussion in outline

The discussion `Waking` is the first discussion of Part I, whose name the chapter `Words` announces. Its topic is a division with no hashes, so its level is zero. The topic's body is empty, since its messages follow its divider at once. Each message gives its author and its words, and the conclusion gives its words apart from the messages.

=>

it.effect('outlines a discussion, its messages, and its conclusion', () =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const outline = yield* outlined('/corpus/waking.loom')
    expect([outline.kind, outline.number, outline.part]).toEqual([
      'Discussion',
      '1',
      { number: 'I', name: 'The room' },
    ])
    const topic = outline.sections[1]
    expect([topic?.name, topic?.level, topic?.body]).toEqual(['What wakes a monster', 0, []])
    expect(topic?.messages).toEqual([
      { author: 'Yegor', text: 'What should wake a monster?' },
      { author: 'Claude', text: "A trap's radius." },
    ])
    expect(topic?.conclusion).toBe('A trap going off wakes it.')
  }),
)

~

## A book loom and a loose loom

The game's book loom names its book and nothing else, so its kind is `Book`, it stands in no part, and its heading gives its title. A loom with no frontmatter is loose, and belongs to the default book, which has no name.

=>

it.effect('tells a book loom and a loose loom', () =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const book = yield* outlined('/corpus/game.loom')
    const loose = yield* outlined('/corpus/notes.loom')
    expect([book.kind, book.title, book.book, book.part, book.number]).toEqual([
      'Book',
      'The game',
      'The game',
      undefined,
      undefined,
    ])
    expect([loose.kind, loose.title, loose.book]).toEqual(['Loose', 'Notes', ''])
  }),
)

~

## A loom that is not there

An application may ask for a loom the workspace does not hold. The outline fails with `NoSuchLoom`, naming the loom asked for.

=>

  it.effect('fails for a loom the workspace does not hold', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const failed = yield* Effect.flip(outlined('/corpus/missing.loom'))
      expect([failed._tag, failed.path]).toEqual(['NoSuchLoom', '/corpus/missing.loom'])
    }),
  )
})

## The concepts of a workspace

The game defines one concept, `Room`, in its book loom, with the other form `rooms`. The opening of the chapter `Words` links it by that form, so the section `Words` is among its uses.

=>

describe('the concepts of a workspace', () => {
  it.effect('lists each concept with the places that use it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const { concepts } = yield* withCompiler(files, (compiler) =>
        compiler.concepts('/corpus/game.loom'),
      )
      expect(
        concepts.map((concept) => [concept.name, concept.forms, concept.path, concept.section]),
      ).toEqual([['Room', ['rooms'], '/corpus/game.loom', 'Room']])
      expect(concepts[0]?.uses).toContainEqual({ path: '/corpus/words.loom', section: 'Words' })
    }),
  )
})

